<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leopold I, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1640">1640</a>–<a href="/years/1705">1705</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leopold I was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> from 1658 to 1705. His reign saw the Ottoman siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a>, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and long wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. To win support he created new princely titles and in 1692 a ninth electorate for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-i-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Leopold I, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Block · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benjamin_von_Block_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leopold was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 9 June 1640, the second son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand III</a> and Maria Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He had been selected for a career in the higher clergy, and the plan was dropped when his elder brother Ferdinand IV died in 1654 and he became heir. His clerical education and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> influence marked him throughout his life: he was unusually knowledgeable for a monarch in theology and jurisprudence and personified the pietas Austriaca of his house. In July 1658 he was elected emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> against the wishes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Cardinal Mazarin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, who had hoped to place the crown on a non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> prince.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In a war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> that began in 1663, Raimondo Montecuccoli won at Saint Gotthard in August 1664, and the Peace of Vasvár brought a twenty years' truce. French expansion, above all the seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> in 1670, drew him into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a>, the Nine Years' War and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, unrest caused partly by his wish to crush <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> led to a rising that was put down in 1671, and the country was treated with great severity for some years. He also expelled the Jewish community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> from the area across the Danube Canal, which was then renamed Leopoldstadt.</p><p>In 1683 an Ottoman army besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> from July to September while Leopold took refuge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a>. On 12 September the allied army, joined by King John III Sobieski of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, routed the Ottoman Turks. Victories followed at Mohács in 1687 and Zenta in 1697, and in January 1699 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> almost the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. In 1687 the Diet of Hungary admitted the right of the Habsburgs to succeed without election. In 1700 he granted the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> the title of King in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. He died on 5 May 1705.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Leopold regarded himself as an absolute sovereign and was extremely tenacious of his rights. He was a staunch proponent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. Short, thin and of sickly constitution, he was cold and reserved in public, with the prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> jaw; a 1670 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coin, which most collectors doubt was accurate, led to his nickname the Hogmouth. He had a taste and talent for music and composed several oratorios and suites of dances, and his sacred music, including a Requiem Mass for his first wife, is perhaps his most successful. He supported composers such as Antonio Bertali and Johann Kaspar Kerll.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leopold I, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The forces of <b>Leopold I, Holy Roman Emperor</b>, under Charles of Lorraine met the army of Sultan Mehmed IV under the Grand Vizier Sarı Süleyman Pasha at Mohács, in the battle also called Harsány Mountain.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-12">12 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke Ernest Augustus of Brunswick-Lüneburg, ruling from Hanover, was granted a ninth electoral dignity by <b>Emperor Leopold</b> I in 1692 in return for military support, though it was recognised by the Imperial Diet only in 1708.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-hanover">Electorate of Hanover</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1692 <b>Emperor Leopold</b> I made Ernest Augustus an elector, and Calenberg became the core of the Electorate of Hanover, which absorbed the Principality of Lüneburg in 1705.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-calenberg">Principality of Calenberg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1599 the family was raised to the rank of imperial counts, and in 1670 Johann Adolf von Schwarzenberg, a senior adviser of <b>Emperor Leopold</b> I, was made a prince of the Empire, with the lands around Scheinfeld forming the core of the family's immediate territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-schwarzenberg">Principality of Schwarzenberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1682" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leopold I, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1682">What was here in 1682? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
